(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 20-year-old man has been using topical tretinoin at night and benzoyl peroxide in the morning, but has begun applying them together and now has stinging and peeling. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Separate the two by at least three hours, because applying them together irritates the skin","Stop the benzoyl peroxide permanently, because it inactivates topical tretinoin on contact","Continue both together and wash the face more often to remove the excess product","Switch both to alternate weeks, because the combination raises photosensitivity risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that interval is specified in the patient education. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a drained furuncle growing methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. He asks why the first antibiotic did not work. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["The organism produces epidermolytic toxins that inactivate oral antibiotics","The empiric agents cephalexin and dicloxacillin do not cover this resistant organism","The organism was not reached because the lesion had not yet been drained at all","The antibiotic was taken for too short a period to eradicate any skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the culture is what redirects therapy to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man is being treated for erysipelas of the leg. Which is the most appropriate supportive management alongside the antibiotic?","choices":["Symptomatic treatment of aches and fever, hydration, cold compresses and elevation of the limb","Warm soaks three times daily with incision if any purulent material has collected","Compression bandaging applied from the toes to the knee for the first week","Dilute acetic acid compresses applied for twenty minutes several times daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with atopic dermatitis of the flexures develops weeping honey-coloured crusting over the affected eczema. Which best describes the infection?","choices":["A primary fungal infection of skin already damaged by another condition","A foreign body reaction within skin already damaged by another condition","A secondary bacterial infection of skin already damaged by another condition","A primary bacterial infection arising in previously normal intact skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bullous impetigo can secondarily invade pre-existing lesions such as eczema. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has not cleared after two weeks of topical clindamycin. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin oral isotretinoin, since follicular disease unresponsive to topicals needs it","Reassure and stop treatment, since folliculitis resolves without any intervention","Unroof a pustule for Gram stain and culture, since this is now a resistant case","Switch to a different topical antibiotic and review again in a further two weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture, potassium hydroxide mount, nasal swab and biopsy are all available. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has impetigo covering much of one arm and both cheeks, too extensive for topical therapy. He has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral doxycycline, which covers both likely organisms","Oral ciprofloxacin, which covers resistant organisms","Oral fluconazole, which covers candidal superinfection","Oral cephalexin, the drug of choice in children"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with diabetes has an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck made of several interconnecting nodules with multiple draining openings, plus fever and chills. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Carbuncle","Furuncle","Cutaneous abscess"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confluent furuncles with separate heads, and systemic symptoms are more common than with a furuncle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has honey-coloured crusted lesions around the nose and mouth that appeared four days after a scratch, and tender submandibular nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Non-bullous impetigo","Bullous impetigo","Herpes simplex of the perioral skin","Ecthyma of the face and perioral area"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a honey-coloured adherent crust on the face with regional lymphadenopathy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has axillary nodules and sinuses. The diagnosis of hidradenitis suppurativa is being considered. Which test is required to confirm it?","choices":["Wood's lamp examination of the axilla, looking for the coral-red fluorescence","None — it is a clinical diagnosis resting on lesions, distribution and recurrence","Bacterial culture of the draining material, since the causative organism guides treatment","Punch biopsy of an active nodule, since the histology is what establishes the diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is not usually required, though it shows follicular occlusion and apocrine gland destruction. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy reports that his acne is worse across the shoulders where his football pads sit. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Anabolic steroid use, which should be asked about in any athlete","Bacterial folliculitis from occlusive clothing worn during training","Acne mechanica, where pressure occludes the pilosebaceous follicles","Acnegenic mineral oil exposure from equipment maintenance products"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — shoulder pads, orthopaedic casts and helmets are the named examples. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has extensive hidradenitis suppurativa in both axillae that has failed medical therapy for years. She asks whether anything can cure it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Spironolactone taken indefinitely gives the best chance of permanent cure","Wide excision of the affected areas gives the best chance of permanent cure","Long-term systemic antibiotic therapy gives the best chance of permanent cure","Laser hair removal of the axillae gives the best chance of permanent cure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large fluctuant cysts are meanwhile incised and drained. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with necrotizing fasciitis of the leg reports that the area, previously exquisitely tender, is now numb. Which explains this change?","choices":["The inflammatory response has begun to resolve with antibiotic therapy","The infection has been walled off by the fascial plane it is spreading in","The analgesia given on admission has reached its full therapeutic effect","The superficial nerves have been destroyed as the infection advanced"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside skin colour changing to blue-grey, bullae and cutaneous gangrene. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man who injects drugs has a tender fluctuant swelling of the forearm that developed at an injection site. Which best describes how this lesion arose?","choices":["Infection of a hair follicle spreading into the adjacent subcutaneous tissue of the arm","Obstruction of an apocrine duct followed by rupture into the surrounding dermal tissue","Spread of streptococci along the superficial lymphatics of the upper dermal layer","Traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the skin, which is how an abscess differs from a furuncle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a furuncle arises from an infected hair follicle instead. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 39-year-old dishwasher is diagnosed with chronic paronychia. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A ten-day course of oral cephalexin covering staphylococci and streptococci","Topical mupirocin to the nail folds until the swelling has fully resolved","Keep the hands as dry as possible and use a broad-spectrum topical antifungal","Warm soaks three times daily and incision if any purulent material collects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with oral fluconazole reserved for severe cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with erysipelas of the leg has a clinically classic presentation. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the leading edge to separate this from contact dermatitis","No routine testing, since blood and tissue cultures have extremely low yield","Blood cultures in every case, since organism identification directs the antibiotic","Computed tomography of the limb to define the depth of tissue involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has redness, swelling and tenderness around the right index fingernail three days after a manicure, with a small collection of pus visible under the nail fold. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute paronychia","Chronic paronychia","Herpetic whitlow","Felon of the fingertip"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — onset two to five days after trauma, starting as cellulitis and progressing to abscess. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 30-year-old primary school teacher has impetigo across one forearm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Defer treatment until swabs of every household contact have been taken and reported","Obtain a culture, because her occupation places her at high risk for resistant organisms","Treat empirically with mupirocin, because culture is never indicated in adult impetigo","Treat empirically with oral doxycycline, because adult impetigo is usually staphylococcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the stated example of that risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with hidradenitis suppurativa has draining sinuses that persist despite topical clindamycin and a mild steroid cream. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Dilute acetic acid compresses applied to the sinuses twice daily for a month","Topical mupirocin applied to the sinus openings three times daily for a week","Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide to decrease the size of the draining sinuses","A three-month course of oral doxycycline with topical benzoyl peroxide added"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral prednisone reduces inflammation and prevents future lesions. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with longstanding athlete's foot develops erysipelas of the same leg. Which is the most appropriate additional management step?","choices":["Take no additional step, since the two conditions are entirely unrelated","Treat the tinea pedis, since it is the portal of entry and predicts recurrence","Begin nasal mupirocin, since staphylococcal carriage drives recurrent disease","Begin oral fluconazole, since the causative organism here is a yeast"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the risk factors for erysipelas. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman with moderate acne has had no improvement on a topical retinoid, benzoyl peroxide and four months of doxycycline. She has regular periods and no hirsutism. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Extend the doxycycline course to twelve months, to give the oral antibiotic more time to take effect","Add a second topical antibiotic to the regimen without any benzoyl peroxide","Reassure her that acne always resolves by the mid-twenties without treatment","Consider a combined oral contraceptive pill, since the acne is unresponsive to both topical and oral therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is one of the two stated indications for hormonal therapy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with folliculitis has failed topical mupirocin. Scraping and culture are being arranged. Which additional test excludes fungal folliculitis?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair","A Wood's lamp examination looking for coral-red fluorescence","A Tzanck smear taken from an unroofed pustule on the thigh","A punch biopsy of normal skin adjacent to an affected follicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the specific test named for dermatophyte folliculitis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with hidradenitis suppurativa asks what he can do himself to reduce flares. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Stop smoking, lose weight, avoid heat and constrictive clothing, and cleanse with an antibacterial wash","Avoid all water contact with the affected areas and apply a barrier ointment twice each day","Isolate from close contacts until treatment has been underway for at least forty-eight hours","Shower promptly after any hot tub use and check the disinfectant level in the water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — smoking cessation is described as essential, and laser hair removal is also offered. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has hot tub folliculitis over the trunk. He is systemically well and the rash began four days ago. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Incision and drainage of the largest pustules with culture of the material","Topical mupirocin three times daily until every lesion has fully resolved","Reassurance with dilute acetic acid compresses, since most clear in two to ten days","Oral ciprofloxacin for a ten-day course to eradicate the organism completely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — 3 tablespoons of 5% vinegar in a pint of water, 20 minutes, two to four times daily. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has three crusted impetigo lesions on one forearm. She is systemically well. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral cephalexin, since impetigo in children always requires systemic antibiotic treatment","Oral doxycycline, since it covers both staphylococcal and streptococcal skin disease","Warm compresses alone, since the condition is self-limiting and resolves without treatment","Mupirocin ointment after removing the crusts, since topical therapy suits limited lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is as effective as oral therapy with fewer side effects. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with pseudofolliculitis barbae is prescribed topical tretinoin. Which is the most appropriate explanation of what it does?","choices":["It kills the Staphylococcus aureus colonising the shaved skin of the beard area","It permanently destroys the follicles so that the hairs will not grow back at all","It softens the hair shaft so that each hair curls away from the skin as it grows","It relieves the hyperkeratosis, removing the epidermis the emerging hair embeds in"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mild corticosteroids reduce inflammation and topical antibiotics reduce colonisation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man treated for erythrasma asks how to stop it returning. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Use a broad-spectrum sunscreen daily and cover the area with clothing outdoors","Keep the area clean and dry, avoid excessive heat and moisture, and maintain a healthy body weight","Avoid sharing towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors with anyone else in the household","Stop smoking and avoid constrictive clothing over the affected skin at all times"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside good general hygiene. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is starting oral isotretinoin for nodular acne. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Pregnancy testing is required before, monthly during and five weeks after treatment, with two forms of contraception preferred","Pregnancy testing is required only before starting, after which monthly liver function tests replace it entirely","Contraception can be stopped as soon as the treatment course has been completed in full","Blood donation is encouraged during treatment because the drug is cleared through the plasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with iPledge enrolment, a one-month supply at a time, and no blood donation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has a rapidly spreading, brightly erythematous, tender plaque on the left shin with an edge that is raised and sharply demarcated. She had a fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius and chills within a day of the rash. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erysipelas","Cellulitis","Contact dermatitis","Deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the raised, sharply demarcated edge with abrupt systemic symptoms is the defining picture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a prosthetic heart valve has a carbuncle requiring drainage. Which additional measure is specifically indicated?","choices":["Nasal mupirocin for five days before the procedure","Blood cultures repeated daily for three days after","Deferral of drainage until an antibiotic has taken effect","Endocarditis prophylaxis before the procedure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is named for at-risk patients in both furuncle and carbuncle management. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with diabetes has a warm, tender, swollen, erythematous area over the right lower leg. The border blends gradually into normal skin. The left leg is unaffected. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Contact dermatitis","Cellulitis","Erysipelas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all four cardinal signs on one lower leg with borders neither raised nor demarcated. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"}]};
